[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Thank you to Incogni for sponsoring this video. On a late afternoon in 2015, a Finnish researcher was staring closely as a bee slowly rolled a ball across a platform. He had spent the past several weeks trying to teach a bee to use a tool, and'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for the first time, it seemed to be working. His name is Professor Olli Loukola, and he has spent the past 10 years studying intelligence and cognition in bumblebees. Through his research, he has found that'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bees are not only able to use tools to obtain a reward, but they\'re even able to solve surprisingly complex puzzles without prior training. In one experiment, bumblebees chose to carry an object through a maze because they seemed to recognize that it would'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'help them access a reward at the end. This type of complex future-oriented thinking has often been considered to be unique to humans and the most intelligent of vertebrates. But Professor Loukola\'s research shows that'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even tiny bees may be capable of this as well. So, what exactly does it mean for a bee to use a tool, and what made Professor Loukola think that a tiny insect might be capable of this in the first place?'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Well, let\'s take a moment to introduce how all of this even began. Olli says he\'s always been fascinated by the animals around him. Even as a'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'toddler, he would sit outside in the garden during the summer wearing nothing but a diaper, staring at ants for hours as they moved across the ground. While other children were playing with toys, he would run his own little experiments like giving ants tiny pieces'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of sugar to observe what they would do. As he grew older, he began spending time on his uncle\'s farm, and it was there that he became interested not just in all kinds of animals, but in the way they behaved.'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That interest followed him into university, where he spent his PhD years doing field work with the great tits and pied flycatchers across Finland. And in 2015, after his PhD, he moved from Finland to join a leading bee'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'research facility in London, the Chitka Lab. Here, they were teaching bees how to pull string to retrieve a flower reward, how to solve puzzles, and investigating how social learning occurred in the first place.'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And before long, Ollie realized that these insects were far more complex than he had ever imagined. As he watched these bees perform such amazing things around him each day, he began to wonder if it was even possible'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for them to know how to use tools. So, next, let\'s take a look together at how he tried to test this. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; To answer this question, we need to'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'understand what scientists mean when they talk about tool use. At its core, tool use is just using a movable object to achieve a specific goal. That can be something as complex as using a stick to pull food closer, or as'

[02:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simple as moving an object to the right place to unlock a reward. With this in mind, Ollie first went to a nearby craft store to try and look for different materials that he could use for his experiment. His first idea was to see whether bees'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'could learn to use a toothpick to get sugar water out of a tube. But he soon realized that although the bees were willing to try, with some of them doing so for up to 20 minutes, the movements required were simply too precise.'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, he realized the tool had to be something the bees could move more easily. In the coming days, Ollie would try showing different objects to the bees. And while Ollie\'s busy designing his experiment, I want to take a moment to'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'introduce today\'s sponsor, Incogni. One of the things that\'s gotten quite ridiculous over the last few years is the amount of scam calls and emails you end up getting every single day. It\'s gotten to the point where I pretty'

