[04:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tended to stay away and not approach them. But one day when he placed a few small wooden balls on the platform, he noticed the bees began rolling them almost immediately. So he decided to build his experiment'

[04:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around that behavior. First, he would teach a bee to push a yellow ball into a hole at the center of the platform by using a plastic demonstrator bee. Once the ball is in the hole, he would open a hatch underneath which would give'

[04:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the bee a sugar reward. He\'ll repeat this several times before placing the bee on the platform with the ball alone. And as he watched closely, he noticed the bee immediately began rolling the ball towards the hole.'

[04:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; I I was like telling to my colleagues like, "Hey, come and see it like I I trained my first bee to roll the ball. They they using the ball as a tool." And then the people were like, "Yeah, yeah, sure. This this you know'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'crazy thing is like doing it doing his crazy stuff." And they they didn\'t believe me, but then when they saw that like, "Wow, this is huge." And they\'re like, "Wow, this is like this is amazing." And Lars was like saying the same like, "Hey Ollie, did'

[05:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you know that this is like this will be big?" &gt;&gt; It was in this moment that Ollie truly fell in love with bees and he couldn\'t help but wonder what else these tiny creatures might be capable of. In the coming weeks he expanded on the'

[05:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'initial study by testing nine more bees. These bees would be given the same training before being tested 10 times on a larger platform. And what he found was that all nine successfully passed each time. And he'

[05:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also noticed that the bees took progressively less time to solve the task over trials. In comparison, he also had a control group that received no training and they failed every time. Now, this might seem like a fairly'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simple experiment, but Ollie was very excited by this and as he watched these bees happily roll the balls across the platform, he started to ponder a far more complex question. How exactly does learning occur in their'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mind? See, humans are able to do something quite remarkable. We are able to learn simply through observation and this is often considered to be an advanced form of social intelligence. So, are bees able to do something'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'similar? For his next experiment, he would have different groups of bees observe the task is solved in three different ways. First, by a demonstrator bee who had previously passed the test. Second, by a ghost demonstrator where he'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'would use a magnet to move the platform underneath the ball. And finally, with no direct demonstration at all. Instead, the bee would find the ball already at the center of the platform.'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In each of these scenarios, the observer bee would be given sugar water as a reward when the ball reaches the center. And there\'s an important detail here. During the demonstrations, there were three balls present but only the'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'furthest ball could be moved by the demonstrators. During the real test, however, any of the three balls could be used. After several demonstrations, the bee students were now ready for the real'

[07:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'test. And what Ollie found was fascinating. First, all the bees that were taught by another bee instructor were able to successfully pass the test pretty much every time. In comparison, the bees that watched'

[07:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only the ghost demonstrator still did surprisingly well, passing around 80% of the time. And finally, the group which had the ball directly placed in the middle still passed around a third of the time.'

[07:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This may not sound like much at first, but it\'s probably the most remarkable result out of them all, because it suggests that the bees may have been able to infer what needed to be done simply by seeing the end result. But, there\'s one more result that was'

[07:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even more impressive. During the demonstrations, the ball that was moved was always the furthest away from the center. However, when the bees actually went to perform the task, most of them went for the ball closest to the center instead.'

[08:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this is fascinating as it shows that the bees weren\'t simply replicating the behavior from the demonstrations, but were actually able to understand how to solve the problem and even improve on the original demonstrations.'

[08:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; In in the test where all these three balls were available, and the observer bee actually didn\'t select the same ball than the demonstrator was using, but they selected the easier or the easiest'

[08:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'option, the ball closest to the goal. And that was the first time uh first evidence that insects can actually uh emulate mhm doing something better based on what'

[08:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they observed someone else doing. &gt;&gt; When this footage aired, it attracted widespread interest from researchers and news outlets all across the world. People quickly fell in love with the'

[08:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'idea of a scientist teaching bees to play mini golf in his lab, and were equally fascinated by what this experiment revealed about their intelligence. Eventually, in 2017, Olli Part ways with the Chittka Lab to start his own lab'

[09:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'back in Finland. And it was there that his research took another major leap forward. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Back in Finland, Olli began thinking'

[09:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about what all of this really meant. He had spent the last few months traveling for lectures and speaking to the press, and he watched as his work spread all around the world. For most people, his work had already shown that bees were capable of far more'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'than anyone had imagined. And yet, his experience over the years made him wonder if they might be capable of even more. This time, the bees will be put through something far more difficult as Ollie'

[09:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wanted to understand if they might even be capable of a form of abstract reasoning. First, the bees will be put through a training phase where they were presented with two different colored balls on a platform.'

[10:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The bees were required to roll the ball which matched the platform\'s color to the center to receive a reward. Ollie also had two additional groups of bees that went through the training with different color combinations. After the bees were able to roll the'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'corresponding ball to the center five consecutive times, they considered to pass the training. And then came the most important part. After the bees had passed the initial training, they were now placed on a new platform with three different colored'

[10:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'balls. Two of these had the same colors that were used during training while the third ball on platform had a matching color that was completely new to the bees. And each bee was given 10 minutes to try and solve the task.'

[10:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This new scenario tested whether the bees had the cognitive flexibility to apply what they had learned to a new situation. Specifically, if the bees would learn to select the color of the ball which corresponds to the new platform even'

[10:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'though they had never seen this color before during training. And what he found was very interesting. The bees were able to solve the task but not in all color combinations. Almost all the bees that were trained on'

[11:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the blue and red platforms were able to pass when they were later tested on the yellow platform. While on the blue platform, none of the bees ended up passing the test with most of them not wanting to even land on the platform itself.'

[11:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And finally, those tested on the red were somewhere in the middle. These results were completely different from what Ollie had expected and in the following weeks, he would train another group of bees to be tested on the blue platform.'

[11:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But again, all of them avoided the blue platform. To try and understand why this was the case, I reached out to Professor Lacola to ask this very question. Why do you think that the bees weren\'t able to pass when they were tested on'

[11:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the blue platform? &gt;&gt; We still don\'t know like what is the what is the reason behind but like I\'m speculating something like um the training history if we train them with the orange and yellow and bees see'

[11:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'orange and yellow uh quite the same like they\'re like in their visual the the yellow and the orange are quite close to each other but then the blue is like on the opposite side. And may maybe it\'s like causing the the'

[12:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'neophobia because it\'s like novel thing, novel color. And that\'s why they\'re like hesitating in the test because now now we know much more about the neophobia behavior or neophobia in bumblebees because of the new paper that we'

[12:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'published in in science where we show that some some bees they even if you let them be in the area like 20 minutes, they rather start sleeping than you know, touching the ball. But then if you pre-train them, they just'

[12:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like happen to know that the ball is something safe and and something that they can move. Then like in the test phase, they start moving the ball almost almost right away when they come into the arena. So it seems there\'s a lot more to'

[12:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'understand about what really goes on in the mind of a bee. But even with that in mind, this experiment suggested something remarkable. That under the right conditions, bees may be able to generalize what they have learned to a completely new situation.'

[13:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And Ollie soon published this in a follow-up paper in 2022. But despite all this, there was still one question left in Ollie\'s mind. All the experiments so far had asked the bee to do the same basic thing.'

[13:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Move a ball into the right place for a reward. But we often use tools in many different ways. And soon, he began to wonder if bees might be able to do something similar.'

[13:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Hey. To test this, Ollie would turn to one of the most famous experiments in animal behavior, the boxing banana experiment. This was an experiment initially done by'

[13:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'German psychologist Wolfgang Köhler in the early 1900s to try and understand how chimpanzees come up with solutions to different problems. Here\'s how it works. First, the chimpanzees were placed in the room'

[13:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which was filled with wooden crates. In the middle of the room was a bunch of bananas suspended high in the air. In Köhler\'s experiment, the chimpanzees wandered around the room for some time and looked frequently between the bananas and the boxes.'

[14:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then almost suddenly, they would start stacking the boxes underneath before climbing up to reach the banana. This study became a classic example not only of creative tool use in animals, but also of their ability for'

[14:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spontaneous problem solving. So, Ollie tried to adapt this for bumblebees. For his experiment, he would begin by placing the bees in a carefully constructed room. In the center was a blue flower with sugar water on top.'

[14:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The bees would enter through a corridor which was slightly obstructed by a Styrofoam ball, and to get to the center, they would need to push this ball out of the way. He would repeat this several times and this taught the bees two things, to'

[14:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'associate the blue flower with a reward and that the Styrofoam ball was safe and easily movable. Now, let\'s take a look at what he found. &gt;&gt; So, we now put the flower from the floor to the ceiling of that arena.'

[14:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And the arena was only 3 cm high, but it was just high enough that the bee couldn\'t fly actually. Bees are quite bumblebees are quite clumsy in flying, so they cannot really'

[15:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fly in that small space. So, the bee saw that flower on the ceiling, and the only way to get the reward was to move the ball underneath the flower and then climb on the ball, and then'

[15:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reach the flower. So, quite difficult task for the bee, right? And here we can see a sample video where the bee is in a in the arena and starting rolling the ball'

[15:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the right well, then climbing on the ball, and reaching the flower. &gt;&gt; But we aren\'t done just yet, because while Ollie had been conducting this experiment, his old colleagues at the Chittka Lab found that bees seem to have'

[15:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a weird obsession with balls. In one experiment, they noticed instead of running straight to food, the bees would choose to stop and roll around with a ball before continuing on, even though there was no incentive provided.'

[15:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That experiment is a story for another time. But when Ollie saw this, he thought that maybe the bees simply solved the task by randomly rolling the ball around the arena. &gt;&gt; If the bee starts rolling the ball in'

[16:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the arena, and then when they\'re coming closer to the flower, the flower becomes bigger in their visual field, and then that\'s kind of driving them to move the ball underneath the flower, and they'

[16:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'might solve the task just by using simple associated mechanisms, which means that yeah, the flower is becoming bigger in their visual field, and they\'re they\'re doing whatever in order to make that flower bigger in their visual field.'

[16:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; So, he decides to take the experiment one step further. He will put a new group of bees through the same training, but this time there was a barrier between the ball and the flower. The bees would initially see the flower'

[16:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when they enter the room, but wouldn\'t have constant visual feedback while they were rolling the ball. He also had a control group of bees that didn\'t go through any training. Now, let\'s take a look together at how the bees performed.'

[16:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is the group that has gone through the initial training, and you can see that the bee cannot see where the flower is when it is initially rolling the ball. Pay very close attention to what the bee does. You can see that she immediately begins'

[17:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rolling the ball towards the flower. She runs into the wall a little on the way, but she manages to get it under the flower and climbs on it to drink nectar. And in the group of just 22 bees, Olli found that around 75% were able to solve'

[17:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the task successfully. In comparison, only 5% were able to pass in the group which received no prior training. And in a later experiment, Olli changed up the shape of the maze so that the'

[17:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bees had to choose which direction to roll the ball in. And again, a significant majority of the bees immediately began rolling the ball towards the area with the blue flower. These experiments marked a major leap'

[17:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from what Olli had found previously, and so just last month in June 2026, Olli published these findings in his most recent paper in Science. In it, he showed that bees may not only be able to use tools in a variety of'

[18:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different ways, but also had the capacity for a level of problem-solving that had previously only been expected from primates. In the end, I couldn\'t help but ask Professor Locola what it was like studying bees all these years, and if'

[18:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there were any interesting stories he might be able to share with us. &gt;&gt; Yeah, of course. It\'s like the most challenging part of this is like you really need like some um you you need to be patient because like some of the bees that they\'re like not,'

[18:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you know, motivated. They they might start sleeping and then and or if you like if you\'re scheduling your day and you say like okay, I will test this bee like, you know, 3:30 and then I need to go home.'

[18:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And then the bee might actually start sleeping in the nest and it\'s not not willing to come. And so yeah, at least if you have like kids and you have to pick up the kids from the school for example,'

[18:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you cannot really like call the school like hey, I need to test this one bee like I will be late like a couple of hours. &gt;&gt; [laughter] &gt;&gt; It doesn\'t work like that. And then like if if it\'s like low pressure outside like the weather is like rainy or'

[19:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cloudy, the bees can, you know, sense that kind of thing inside the lab and they\'re like sleepy as well. Like they like because in the nature if it\'s rainy, they they won\'t go out foraging that often. So, these kind of things that you if you'

[19:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'don\'t know these things, you you might like lose your mind because you\'re like, "Oh, what is going on here?" All the bees are sleeping. And I was like, Like many times my students ask me these questions. I\'m like, "Hey, look outside, it\'s raining." Yeah, like'

[19:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if you start bumblebee stuff from the scratch, you will lose your mind because so many different things that might actually &gt;&gt; [laughter] [music] &gt;&gt; Ollie has often wondered why we seem so'

[19:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'determined to place one species above another. For decades, intelligence was often treated as something hierarchical with humans at the top and other animals arranged below [music] according to how closely they resembled our abilities.'

[19:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But Ollie has always seen things differently. [music] He believes that every species has their own strengths that allow them to navigate their world. And over the years, he has been glad [music] to see'

[20:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that public perception is slowly beginning to shift in that direction, too. In recent years, science has increasingly revealed that intelligence can take [music] shape in more forms than we once imagined. And through Ollie\'s own research, he has'

[20:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shown that even tiny insects like bees can learn to use tools and solve complex problems in ways that many people once thought was impossible. When Ollie reflects on his research over the years, he believes that one of his'

[20:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'greatest strengths was his own naivety. Because he hadn\'t spent years learning what bees couldn\'t do, he allowed himself to ask the strange questions [music] and try ideas others may have dismissed too quickly.'

[20:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In the end, that\'s [music] the advice he gives not only to researchers, but to anyone trying to understand the world a little better. To stay open-minded [music] and to not be afraid of having ideas that may seem a little ridiculous at'

[20:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first. And for Ollie, this is only the beginning. Questions about consciousness, emotion, [music] and pain in insects are still heavily debated, but with new tools like machine learning and motion tracking, we'

[21:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'now have increasingly precise [music] ways of measuring behavior, and Ollie believes we\'re getting closer to answering those questions than ever before. So, what\'s the next step [music] in'

[21:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'terms of your research? Can you share anything with us about your future plans? &gt;&gt; And now we have some like uh new plans to kind of continue with the use of the similar kind of setup, but now we will measure'

[21:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the motivational levels of the bees when, you know, for example, when they have the goal in their mind, they know that yeah, the blue flower rewarding flower is over there. There\'s a ball, maybe I should use that as a tool or stepping stool.'

